## 2. Dadaism (1916–1924): The Critique of Meaning

### 2.1 Core Principles

Dada emerged in Zurich during World War I as a radical rejection of the rationalism and nationalism that had led to war. Its founders—Hugo Ball, Tristan Tzara, Richard Huelsenbeck, Hans Richter—sought to destroy the very concept of art and meaning.

**Key principles of Dada:**

| Principle | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| **Anti-art** | Rejection of traditional aesthetic values |
| **Absurdity** | Embrace of nonsense, irrationality, chance |
| **Language deconstruction** | Sound poetry, cut-up techniques, meaningless texts |
| **Shock** | Provocation, scandal, public outrage |

**Tristan Tzara's Dada Manifesto (1918):**
> "I am against systems; the most acceptable system is to have none."

### 2.2 What Post-Perception Inherits from Dada

| Inheritance | From Dada | In Post-Perception |
|-------------|-----------|-------------------|
| **Shock as a tool** | Public provocation and scandal | Perceptual shock through sensory contradiction |
| **Deconstruction of language** | Sound poetry, cut-up techniques | Deconstruction of linguistic authority ("This is a Pipe") |
| **Anti-institutional stance** | Attack on museums, galleries, academic art | Critique of institutional power ("This is Not a Urinal") |

### 2.3 The Rupture: From Nihilism to Perceptual Training

The crucial difference between Dada and Post-Perception lies in their **goals**:

| Dimension | Dada | Post-Perception |
|-----------|------|-----------------|
| **Goal** | Destruction of meaning, celebration of absurdity | Perceptual training, development of immunity |
| **Attitude** | Nihilistic, negative | Constructive, immunizing |
| **Outcome** | "Art is nonsense" | "Perception is fragile; train it" |

Dada destroyed meaning but offered nothing in its place. Post-Perception deconstructs perception in order to **rebuild** it—to train it, to immunize it, to make it more resistant to deception.

**As the Post-Perception manifesto declares:** "Destruction is not an end in itself; it is a necessary moment of deconstruction before reconstruction."

