## 4. Conceptual Art (1960s–1970s): The Primacy of the Idea

### 4.1 Core Principles

Conceptual Art emerged in the 1960s as a radical shift from the aesthetic object to the idea. Sol LeWitt's "Paragraphs on Conceptual Art" (1967) declared that "the idea becomes the machine that makes the art."

**Key principles of Conceptual Art:**

| Principle | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| **Idea over object** | The concept is more important than the physical work |
| **Dematerialization** | Art can exist as documentation, instruction, or text |
| **Language as medium** | Words, definitions, and texts become the artwork |
| **Critique of the art object** | Rejection of the commodity status of art |

**Sol LeWitt:**
> "In conceptual art the idea or concept is the most important aspect of the work... The idea becomes the machine that produces the art."

### 4.2 What Post-Perception Inherits from Conceptual Art

| Inheritance | From Conceptual Art | In Post-Perception |
|-------------|---------------------|-------------------|
| **Primacy of the idea** | The concept is central | The philosophical framework is foundational |
| **Language as medium** | Texts, definitions, instructions | The principle "Language is Power" |
| **Dematerialization** | Documentation, instructions | The experience itself is the work (not the physical artifact) |

### 4.3 The Rupture: From the Idea to Perception

The crucial difference lies in **what** the artwork investigates:

| Dimension | Conceptual Art | Post-Perception |
|-----------|----------------|-----------------|
| **Subject** | The concept, the idea | Perception, sensory experience |
| **Focus** | "What is art?" | "How do we perceive?" |
| **Viewer's role** | Interpreter of the idea | Test subject of perception |
| **Example** | Kosuth's "One and Three Chairs" (the idea of a chair) | "This is a Pipe" (the perception of a pipe) |

Conceptual Art asks: "What is art?" Post-Perception asks: "How do we see?" One is about the definition of art; the other is about the fragility of perception.

